<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John William Friso</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1687">1687</a>–<a href="/years/1711">1711</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as John William Friso of Nassau-Dietz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John William Friso was prince of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diez">Dietz</a> and stadtholder of Friesland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a>. In 1702 he inherited part of William III's estate and took the title Prince of Orange, though the inheritance was disputed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. He drowned in 1711 crossing the Hollandsch Diep at Moerdijk, and his posthumous son became William IV, ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> royal house.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-william-friso.jpg" alt="John William Friso" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lancelot Volders · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lancelot_Volders_-_John_William_Friso,_Prince_of_Orange.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
